  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let $P$ be the matrix that represents the rotation and let $Q$ be the matrix that represents the stretch. These are given by the following elementary transformation matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & -\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(-\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) & \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(-\\frac{5\\pi }{4})\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ Q & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the combined transformation can be represented by the matrix $QP:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}QP & =(\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}])(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
